  "verse": 42,
  "plain_english": "`pilot_V3I_ARGUMENT_MAP.md`. Dyczkowski-mode.*\n\n**The will as cause, agent, and action**\n\nThe whole vimarśa has argued that causality is the conscious knower's agency. Now comes the sharpest\nobjection of all, and it threatens to undo everything. If consciousness is *one* — if all the world is\na single consciousness and the difference of things is only ignorance — then how is action possible at\nall? For action needs an agent and an object, two distinct things; but if all is one, there is no\ndistinct object for the agent to act upon.\n\nAbhinavagupta's answer is that action is the one *will-to-do* that joins the agent and the object. The\njar and the consciousness are, by appearance, two. But there is a single re-recollection (parāmarśa)\nmarked by the will-to-do — the moment \"I will make this\" — which rests, as it were, in the other\nconsciousness-essence and binds the two together. It is this one will-recollection, and not any\nexternal contact, that makes the jar an object of action and consciousness its agent. Without it there\nis no action, even in a perfectly unified consciousness.\n\nBut here Abhinavagupta turns on the very idea that the difference is a mere \"ignorance.\" If\nconsciousness is one, and the difference is ignorance, then we must ask: *of whom* is this ignorance?\nThe one-form Brahman cannot have an ignorance that is the mere absence of something else. Nor can the\nseparate souls exist — they too would be raised up by the ignorance. If the ignorance is called\n\"un-explainable\" (anirvācya), then we still do not know whose it is; \"it shines and yet is\nun-explainable\" is the speech of a yakṣa, a being we cannot pin down. And in fact the difference is\nreal: our cognition rests in difference as it does not rest in non-difference, and the very \"refutation\"\nthat would remove difference is itself a manifestation — and so would be difference, and so would be\nignorance. The whole attempt to dismiss the world as an un-explainable illusion cuts the horns of the\nhare's head and plucks the sky-flower. Even if we appeal to the revealed teaching, that teaching too\nwould be ignorance — a net of illusions. (And indeed, as Abhinavagupta will say, \"even Brahman with\nignorance is [admitted by some], so this is not an absolute monism either.\")\n\nWhat, then, of the division of the powers — will in the Lord, knowledge in Sadāśiva, action in Īśvara?\nThis is not a separation of real things; it is one action-power, seen under different aspects. The\naction-power in the action is nothing other than the will. As the teaching says, \"the three subtle\npowers dwell in harmony.\" And the grammarians' own analysis confirms it: the action-tattva is seen in\nthe agent even when the other adjuncts are absent, and is not seen when the agent's complement is\nabsent — so by presence-and-absence, it is the *resting in the agent* alone that is the action. Even\nwhere no explicit agent appears — \"here it does not cook\" — the agents fit for cooking and enjoying are\nmanifest, implied by the locus. Action is not a thing that happens to an inert object; it is the will\nat rest in the knower.\n\nAnd so the final synthesis: the will (icchā) itself is the cause-ness, the agent-ness, and the action.\nThe three are one act of consciousness. The cause does not cause, the agent does not act, the action\ndoes not move — all three are the one will, by which the Lord, wishing to stand, becomes the world of\njar and cloth and all the appearances. This is the action-power: the will of the one consciousness,\nwhich is at once what causes, what acts, and what is done. With this, the fourth vimarśa of the\nkriyādhikāra — the reflexive-awareness of the cause-effect relation — comes to its close.\n\n- **Preserved:** the objection (one consciousness → no distinct agent/object → no action); the answer\n  (the one will-recollection cikīrṣā-parāmarśa joins agent and object); the genitive joining; the\n  turn on \"ignorance\" (of whom? no separate jīvas; anirvācya = yakṣa-speech); the cognition-rests-in-\n  difference point; the bādhā-is-itself-difference reductio; the hare's-horn/sky-flower; the āgama-\n  too-is-ignorance; the \"brahmāpīṣṭam avidyayā saha\" (not absolute monism); the akasmāt (no cause\n  found); the kriyātva not in Brahman; the Himalaya-devatātma nanu and answer (metaphorical); the need\n  for the aiśvaryaśakti; the icchā/jñāna/kriyā division as one power; the sāmānādhikaraṇya; the\n  śi. dṛ. 1.4; the grammarians' anvaya-vyatireka (rest-in-agent is the action); the \"here it does not\n  cook\" implied agents; the icchā = cause-ness/agent-ness/action; the ||4|| colophon.\n- **Added (SUPPLIED, recoverable):** \"the moment 'I will make this'\" (glosses cikīrṣā-parāmarśa);\n  \"it binds the two together\" (restates the joining); \"the whole attempt … cuts the horns of the\n  hare's head\" (states the reductio). Interpretive unpacking, not new doctrine.\n- **NOT resolved:** the exact \"yakṣa-bhāṣā\" (speech of a yakṣa) image; the precise force of the\n  \"sambandhinī\" supplied word in the Vṛtti. Logged, not invented.\n\n**Verdict:** understood and readable. The vimarśa closes with a clean, powerful argument: action is\npossible in the one consciousness because it is the one will-recollection joining agent and object;\nthe \"un-explainable ignorance\" is a reductio; and the will itself is cause, agent, and action — the\nwhole causal structure is one act of consciousness.",
  "sanskrit_cite": "tathābhūta | (page | 240) | eva | jātaḥ | iti | na | asya | darpaṇarūpatā | sattvam | | | nanu",
